  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nPotent upper level troughing moving across the region today will \nbring an array of impacts, including strong to severe thunderstorms \nand elevated fire weather conditions. A cold front will be moving \nthrough today, which will delineate the transition between a more \nthunderstorm focused risk and fire weather risk. \n\nAhead of the cold front ample moisture looks to coincide with \ndaytime heating and forcing from the passing upper level disturbance \nto result in scattered showers and thunderstorms, mainly east of I-\n15. Deep mixing and shear on the order of 30-40kts will support the \nrisk for large hail with initial storms before the risk transitions \nto be more wind focused. Latest mesoanalysis features DCAPE in \nexcess of 1200 J/Kg over the plains, which would support the risk \nfor wind gusts approaching 80 mph in the strongest thunderstorms \nthat form. While PWATs are still elevated, the deep inverted-V \nprofiles will support a mix of wet and dry thunderstorms. \n\nBehind the cold front a period of breezy west winds is forecast to \ndevelop in most areas. The lowest portion of the atmosphere dries \nout quite quickly behind the front, which will allow for relative \nhumidity to continue falling behind the front, even as temperatures \ncool. The combination of the breezy and dry conditions, as well as \nthe potential for a few dry thunderstorms brings about an elevated \nfire weather risk across most of the region. \n\nHeading into Monday the upper system responsible for active weather \ntoday will exit eastward. Weak ridging builds in across the west in \nwake of this system, though with some lingering monsoonal moisture \nnear the Idaho border between Monida and West Yellowstone, a few \nthunderstorms cannot be ruled out by late afternoon. The rest of the \nregion looks dry Monday. \n\nThe upper level ridging more or less persists much of the upcoming \nweek, but as it shifts slowly eastward an upper level trough \napproaching the Pacific NW will slowly allow for showers and \nthunderstorms to spread further and further northward across the \nregion each day. -AM \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThe main source of uncertainty today is with respect to how ongoing \nconvection over the plains north of Great Falls will modify the \nconvective environment this afternoon. On one hand this activity may \nlimit destabilization in some areas where forcing is greatest. \nConversely this activity and associated outflow may serve as a focus \nfor additional convective initiation mid-afternoon, resulting in \nperhaps a greater areal extent of convective activity.\nADditionally, if this activity persists, it could eventually break\nthe cap and intensify and become the main area of concern across\nthe Hi-Line. Nonetheless, any thunderstorm that forms this \nafternoon will be capable of strong wind gusts, and at least \nbriefly large hail. -AM",
